<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu was a French naval officer and Carmelite friar who joined de Gaulle's Free French in 1940 and rose to admiral. As High Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> from 1945 to 1947 he worked to restore French rule, proclaiming the Autonomous Republic of Cochinchina in June 1946 while talks with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> were under way. His hard line contributed to the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-thierry-dargenlieu.jpg" alt="Georges Thierry d'Argenlieu" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Conrad Poirier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:News._Commandant_Thierry_d%27Argenlieu_BAnQ_P48S1P07042.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
